$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1e163caaf670da17583312ca"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\binom n1$?","options":["$n$","$1$","$0$","$n^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"057ec45c0a4e6b915c5c7036"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In a binomial probability, what does $\\binom nk$ count?","options":["Which $k$ trials were successes","The probability of one specific success pattern","The number of possible probability functions","The number of failures after successes are ignored"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b203c1c08c8836f0bc9e919a"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $\\binom nk$ a combination count rather than a permutation count?","options":["It arranges every object in a sequence","It ignores the order of the selected objects","It requires repeated selections","It assigns probabilities to events"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3169cab57c85215df7810e53"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which fact explains the boundary value $\\binom nn=1$?","options":["There are no ways to choose all objects","Choosing all objects records $n!$ possible orders","There is exactly one way to choose all $n$ objects","The value follows only from probability axioms"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9f50039a4acbcaf69703933"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_013","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The sum $\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom nk$ equals:","options":["$2n$","$2^{n}$","$n!$","$n^{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d3cfbef9f8dac015edf77453"},{"id":"pt_batch3_binomial_coefficient_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Use symme